### Key commands
```
{{ if .Env.CABundleConfigMap -}}
# Create the issuer CA bundle configmap ({{ .Env.PKI }} PKI chain):
oc create configmap {{ .Env.CABundleConfigMap }} \
  --from-file=ca-bundle.crt={{ .Env.PKI }}-pki-chain.crt -n openshift-config

{{ end -}}
# Create the console client secret (confidential client):
oc create secret generic {{ .Env.ConsoleSecretName }} \
  --from-literal=clientSecret=<console-client-secret> -n openshift-config

# Apply the generated Authentication CR:
oc apply -f authentication-cr.yaml

# Watch the rollout:
oc get authentication.config/cluster -o jsonpath='{.spec.type}{"\n"}'   # -> OIDC
oc get clusteroperator kube-apiserver

# Validate with a {{ .Env.SmartCard }} smart card inserted:
oc login   # uses the oc-oidc exec plugin against {{ .Env.IssuerURL }}
```

## Username mapping → RBAC
This deployment maps the OpenShift username from the `{{ .Env.UsernameClaim }}` claim. Because
no username prefix is configured (`prefixPolicy: NoOpinion`), OpenShift may prefix a username
that does not come from the `sub` claim with the issuer URL — e.g. `{{ .Env.IssuerURL }}#<value>`.
After the first login, run `oc whoami` to see the exact username string, and make your
`RoleBinding` / `ClusterRoleBinding` subjects match it.

